1
00:00:00,216 --> 00:00:01,999
Could you fly four astronauts to

2
00:00:02,009 --> 00:00:02,651
the Moon...

3
00:00:02,661 --> 00:00:04,278
and bring them back alive?

4
00:00:04,794 --> 00:00:05,995
We built AstroByte:

5
00:00:06,005 --> 00:00:07,873
a game where your team designs

6
00:00:07,883 --> 00:00:08,902
a space mission,

7
00:00:08,912 --> 00:00:09,910
part by part.

8
00:00:10,326 --> 00:00:11,367
Four roles.

9
00:00:11,377 --> 00:00:11,892
Mass,

10
00:00:11,902 --> 00:00:12,505
power,

11
00:00:12,515 --> 00:00:13,993
budget and data,

12
00:00:14,003 --> 00:00:15,044
in tension.

13
00:00:15,054 --> 00:00:16,270
If one fails,

14
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:18,181
there is no launch.

15
00:00:18,597 --> 00:00:20,685
Every part comes from real data:

16
00:00:20,695 --> 00:00:21,512
instruments,

17
00:00:21,522 --> 00:00:22,084
rockets,

18
00:00:22,094 --> 00:00:24,152
antennas and ground stations.

19
00:00:24,568 --> 00:00:26,356
When the whole team approves...

20
00:00:26,366 --> 00:00:27,022
liftoff!

21
00:00:29,188 --> 00:00:31,433
And mission control erupts with joy.

22
00:00:32,599 --> 00:00:34,633
The trajectory isn't a drawing:

23
00:00:34,643 --> 00:00:35,846
it's real physics.

24
00:00:35,856 --> 00:00:37,890
A figure eight around the Moon,

25
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:39,231
validated with GMAT,

26
00:00:39,241 --> 00:00:40,477
NASA's software.

27
00:00:40,893 --> 00:00:43,153
The cameras photograph the far side,

28
00:00:43,163 --> 00:00:44,724
using real lunar data.

29
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:46,093
And the way home:

30
00:00:46,103 --> 00:00:47,163
three parachutes...

31
00:00:47,173 --> 00:00:48,982
and splashdown in the Pacific.

32
00:00:50,548 --> 00:00:51,334
At the end,

33
00:00:51,344 --> 00:00:53,855
an analysis explains what went right,

34
00:00:53,865 --> 00:00:55,249
what failed and why,

35
00:00:55,259 --> 00:00:56,404
graded A to F.

36
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:58,339
And you can explore the space

37
00:00:58,349 --> 00:00:58,746
center,

38
00:00:58,756 --> 00:01:00,071
ride up the tower and see

39
00:01:00,081 --> 00:01:01,403
the rocket on the pad.

40
00:01:01,819 --> 00:01:03,721
We are Team AstroByte,

41
00:01:03,731 --> 00:01:05,882
for NASA Space Apps 2026.

42
00:01:05,892 --> 00:01:07,793
Subscribe and tell me:

43
00:01:07,803 --> 00:01:10,530
what mission would you design?

